@h1 Data access h1@

Data access reaches into a record or collection from prose:
@@@code @name.field code@@@, @@@code @collection.0.field code@@@, and deeper
chains. It is the @strong verified strong@ way to put data into a sentence —
not @@@code {{…}} code@@@, which does not interpolate in prose.

@h2 Fields and indices h2@

Follow the handle with a dot and a field name. The chain reads segments until it
hits a byte that is not part of one — a sentence-final period ends it cleanly:

@@@pre
#keeper: { name - Ada }

The keeper was @keeper.name.
pre@@@

Collections index by @strong zero-based number strong@:

@@@pre
#findings: [ { claim - A, strength - high }, { claim - B, strength - low } ]

First: @findings.0.claim. Second strength: @findings.1.strength.
pre@@@

(fixtures 11-data-access/field-access, index-access,
access-into-collection-of-records.) Chains go as deep as the data:
@@@code @keeper.history.years code@@@ (access-into-nested-record) and
@@@code @x.y.z.w code@@@ (deep-chain).

@h2 Fuzzy key matching h2@

Keys match by @strong canonical form strong@: lowercase the key, then keep only
@code [a-z0-9] code@ — spaces, hyphens, and underscores drop out, and camelCase
collapses. So a field stored as @code years at post code@ is reachable by any
space-free spelling:

@@@pre
#keeper: { years at post - 31 }

camel @keeper.yearsAtPost, snake @keeper.years_at_post
pre@@@

Both resolve to @code 31 code@ (verified; fixtures fuzzy-match-camel,
fuzzy-match-snake). If two different keys collapse to the same canonical form,
the access is @code E_AMBIGUOUS_RECORD_KEY code@.

@h2 A space ends the segment h2@

Here is the sharp edge that contradicts an old example comment: @strong a space
terminates the access segment strong@. So a multi-word key written with spaces
does @em not em@ resolve:

@@@pre
#keeper: { years at post - 31 }

He served @keeper.years at post here.
pre@@@

Only @@@code @keeper.years code@@@ is read — it is unresolved, and @code at post
code@ trails as prose (verified; fixture fuzzy-match-spaces). @strong Multi-word
keys are reachable only through the space-free camel or snake forms above. strong@

@h2 Missing keys degrade, not fail h2@

A missing field is @strong not a parse error strong@ — it is resolved at expand
time and rendered as an unresolved marker
(@code <span class="wit-unresolved"> code@), so a typo shows up in the output
rather than breaking the build (fixture missing-field).

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Typing spaces into an access, like @@@code @keeper.years at post code@@@ —
use the camel or snake form. li@
@li Expecting a missing field to fail the build — it degrades gracefully at
expand time. li@
@li Reaching for @@@code {{…}} code@@@ to read data in prose — use
@@@code @field code@@@. li@
ul@

